ny neighbor in prev layer gives distance ≤i, so distance = i if no path shorter; edges only same/adjacent means any path from root to Vi must pass layers sequentially, so ≥i). Good.\n\nCounting by layer sizes: For transition from current layer a at parity layer i to new layer b at i+1, choose b vertices from remaining rem: C(rem,b). Edges between layers i and i+1: for each new vertex, choose subset of a previous vertices, with none empty: inclusion-exclusion (y+1)^a -1? Number of ways weighted by number edges x: ((1+x)^a -1)^b = sum_j (-1)^{b-j} C(b,j)(1+x)^{a j}. Expanding in y=1+x gives T. Their terms: sum_j (-1)^j C(b,j) y^{a(b-j)}. Same with j→b-j. So correct. Edges within new layer: 2^{C(b,2)} y exponent C(b,2), yes all arbitrary. Edges within current layer counted when it was added; root none. Edges between nonadjacent layers forbidden. What about edges within current layer to previous? already counted. So each labeled graph counted exactly once based on its BFS layer sets. Good.\n\nDP details of polynomial shift and multiplication:\nT terms exponent a*(b-j)+base. For each term, dest[i+sh] += poly[i]*coef*C(rem,b). This corresponds multiplying by y^sh. Yes because T is polynomial in y. dp polynomials track edges count in y=1+x? Wait they track polynomial in y, where y = 1+x. Each edge factor y. Total graph polynomial in y = sum graphs y^{#edges} = sum_M count_M (1+x)^M. Then after full DP R(y)=sum_M c_M y^M. Conversion: coefficient of x^M in R(1+x) = sum_{d≥M} R[d] C(d,M). They compute for each M. Yes.\n\nRoot vertex? dp[1] e=1, a=1,p=0 poly [1]. Layer0 has 1 even. Good.\n\nCould graphs have layer count > n? cap ensures even layer total <=n and odd <=n. Final N even. Good.\n\nOne subtle: They only iterate t in range(1,N), thus transitions where t+b=N happen when t<N, included. dp[N] has final layers. They don't allow extending beyond N. Good.\n\nPotential correctness for N=2 and N=4 checked.\n\nPotential performance with P around 1e8 and modular multiplication